//< `defer` holds no capture list (`defer a, b { ... }`)
//<
//< A `defer` takes a block or one statement, and its body reads the values **at the point of
//< leaving** (docs/DESIGN.md §4.2): the real uses are almost all `.free()`/`dealloc`/`fclose`,
//< which capture at registration invalidates. Copy into a separate binding to keep such a value.
// ==========================================
// `defer n` reads as the one-statement form, so the refusal comes at the `{` behind it.
// EXPECT: expected ';'
// EXPECT: parse/syntax_error
// Where the user's source is merely invalid, do not show ksplc's own `?` propagation road
// (print_internal_trace in ksplc/main.kspls).
// EXPECT-NOT: stack backtrace
// ==========================================
fn main() {
    $n: Sz = 1;
    defer n { _x: = n; }
    n = 2;
}